/* E1 — Tariff shock what-if: THE AUDIT TRAIL, NOT THE ANSWER.
 *
 * READ THIS BEFORE QUOTING ANY NUMBER THIS QUERY RETURNS.
 *
 * This is the theme-level formulation, and it is retired as an answer. It reaches
 * a sector through pre-computed Theme->Sector `correlatesWith` edges, which are a
 * corpus-wide summary rather than anything conditioned on the analogs retrieved.
 * Theme->Sector is complete bipartite (16 x 11 x 4 = 704 edges), so EVERY analog
 * reaches EVERY sector and the ranking is identical whichever analogs come back.
 * Change the question, get the same answer. The cited episodes are decorative.
 *
 * It is kept, and kept runnable, because the demo shows this retirement rather
 * than describing it — and because the path output is a genuinely good audit
 * trail. Use it to answer "how did this system reach Energy at all", never
 * "how much does Energy move".
 *
 * WHAT REPLACED IT, for the number itself:
 *   - app/queries.py OUTCOME/SWEEP — computes the move from the price series at
 *     the analog dates themselves, so the answer moves when the question does.
 *   - Visualizer editor "GDELT · Class vs instance" — the current headline result.
 *
 * And what that replacement found, so nobody re-derives it from this file: there
 * is NO detectable sector effect from a routine tariff announcement. Swept across
 * every dyad, CAMEO code, horizon and target in the 21.3M-event corpus, 405,987
 * tests: NOT ONE survives correction for a search that wide, the strongest missing
 * the bar by 3.4x (scripts/multiple_testing.py). Specific large shocks are
 * measurable (Saudi-Russia price war, WTI -24.59%, z = -8.5); event CLASSES are
 * not. That contrast is the finding.
 *
 * THE CAUSAL CHAIN. The event-study edges do NOT hang off Event. They hang off
 * Theme (correlatesWith: Theme -> Sector/Index/Commodity, 1,088 edges) and off
 * Commodity/MacroVariable (affects: -> Sector, 77). So an analog reaches a sector as:
 *
 *     Event -taggedWith-> Theme -correlatesWith-> Sector
 *                               \-correlatesWith-> Commodity -affects-> Sector
 *
 * Traversing correlatesWith straight out of an Event returns zero rows.
 *
 * WITH is mandatory here on a cluster: the traversal reaches vertex
 * collections (Theme, Sector, Index, Commodity) that are not named in the
 * traversal loop itself — only the edge collections are. Without it the query
 * fails at EXECUTION time (error 1521), not at parse time, so it can look fine
 * in review and die during the demo.
 *
 * Bind vars: @cameo (e.g. "163" impose embargo/restrict), @actorFrom "USA",
 *            @actorTo "CHN", @topK. There is no @themes — an earlier header
 *            documented one the body never used, and passing it fails with
 *            "bind parameter 'themes' was not declared".
 */
WITH Event, Theme, Sector, Index, Commodity, Actor

// 1) Analog events: same CAMEO action, same dyad. (Stage 3 replaces this with
//    faceted ArangoSearch scoring over the event_analog view + vector re-rank.)
LET analogs = (
  // One event per DATE, not the top-k by volume. Ranking purely on numMentions
  // collapses 55 available dates onto 4, because coverage is concentrated on the
  // biggest news days (1,818 mentions on the leader, 78 on the eighth). Taking the
  // best-supported event per date spans 2018-03..2020-01 instead of two episodes.
  // Measured 2026-08-21. Stage 2 replaces this with the faceted
  // scorer over `event_analog`, keeping date diversity as a constraint on top.
  FOR ev IN Event
    FILTER ev.cameo == @cameo
    FILTER ev.actorFromCode == @actorFrom AND ev.actorToCode == @actorTo
    COLLECT date = ev.dateAdded INTO g
    LET best = FIRST(FOR x IN g[*].ev SORT x.numMentions DESC RETURN x)
    SORT best.numMentions DESC
    LIMIT @topK
    RETURN best
)

// 2) Each analog -> its themes -> the assets those themes move.
FOR ev IN analogs
  FOR theme, tagEdge IN 1..1 OUTBOUND ev taggedWith
    FOR asset, corr, path IN 1..2 OUTBOUND theme correlatesWith, affects
      OPTIONS { uniqueVertices: "path", bfs: true }
      FILTER IS_SAME_COLLECTION("Sector", asset)
      // The last hop carries the event-study statistics.
      LET stat = path.edges[-1]
      COLLECT sector = asset.name INTO hits = {
        move: stat.move,
        horizon: stat.horizonDays,
        consistency: stat.consistency,
        analog: ev._key,
        analogDate: ev.dateAdded,
        theme: theme.name,
        articleSupport: tagEdge.articleCount,
        path: path                     // the actual subgraph used
      }
      LET moves = (FOR h IN hits FILTER h.move != null RETURN h.move)
      SORT AVERAGE(moves) DESC
      RETURN {
        sector,
        n: LENGTH(hits),
        expectedMove: AVERAGE(moves),
        range: [MIN(moves), MAX(moves)],
        typicalHorizonDays: AVERAGE(hits[*].horizon),
        analogs: UNIQUE(hits[*].analog),   // cited episodes (auditability)
        themes: UNIQUE(hits[*].theme),     // why this sector was reached
        paths: hits[*].path                // the supporting subgraph
      }
